[Unit]
Description=XYZO weather archive - keep a copy of every GFS analysis cycle
After=network.target postgresql.service xyzo-windmap.service xyzo-tempmap.service xyzo-isobars.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# The database password lives in the root-only env file, not in the
# unit. A unit file is world-readable; a committed one is a published
# password.
EnvironmentFile=/etc/xyzo/xyzo.env
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 -m worker.weather_archive --archive
User=asher
WorkingDirectory=/var/www/vhosts/xyzo.org

# WHAT IT DOES. The three weather workers publish the wind grid, the
# temperature grid and the pressure contours, and each of them REPLACES
# the previous cycle when the next one lands. This worker copies the
# published files into data/weather_archive/<cycle>/, so "what did the
# weather look like on 3 March last year?" has an answer.
#
# IT COPIES, IT DOES NOT RE-COMPUTE. The archive is a copy of what the
# site is already serving — there is no second implementation of a field.
#
# WHY EVERY 15 MINUTES. The analyses are six-hourly, but the three
# workers publish on their own timers and their cycles can differ by one
# analysis, so the archive polls: it archives the cycle that is standing
# right now, and it does nothing if that cycle is already stored.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS. 369 KB a cycle — the 1° wind grid, the 1° temperature
# grid and the classic 4 hPa pressure chart, gzipped — so a year is
# about 550 MB. The 0.25° grids would be 8.8 GB a year, which is why
# the archive keeps the 1° ones (the same grids the 3D globe draws).
#
# The archive lives OUTSIDE the web root and outside git
# (data/weather_archive/): it is a runtime artefact, like
# data/nn_models/, and it is served through the API rather than as
# static files.
